<DocsLayout title="Operations" description="Operate, observe, and safely evolve super-charge-ml workflows." current="operations">
  <p class="eyebrow"><span></span> Shipping with evidence</p>
  <h1>Observe the run.<br /><em>Earn the release.</em></h1>
  <p class="lead">A production workflow is more than a serving endpoint. It must be resumable, inspectable, authenticated, monitored, and reversible.</p>

  <h2>Operational loop</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Measure:</strong> capture coverage, benchmark results, resource use, and data-quality findings.</li>
    <li><strong>Observe:</strong> retain events, manifests, fingerprints, bundle checksums, and monitoring reports.</li>
    <li><strong>Evaluate:</strong> compare candidates on locked data and compare live behavior with the champion.</li>
    <li><strong>Evolve:</strong> turn recurring findings into tests, validation rules, benchmarks, or focused changes.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Safe recovery</h2>
  <p>Interrupted or failed runs preserve checkpoints and recoverable trials. Resume only with the same configuration and data identity; stale checkpoints are refused.</p>
  <pre><code>scml resume runs/&lt;run-id&gt;</code></pre>

  <h2>Durable state</h2>
  <p>Run evidence, incremental checkpoints, source registries, observability retention files, fault evidence, and signing-key rotation use a temporary file, flush its contents, atomically replace the destination, and flush the containing directory. This protects both the bytes and the rename across a sudden process or power failure. It does not make a remote object store transactional; distributed transports still need their own commit protocol.</p>

  <h2>Bundle security</h2>
  <p>Sign bundles with <code>SCML_SIGNING_KEY</code> and verify them at load time. Rotate keys without reserializing the model:</p>
  <pre><code>ModelBundle.rotate_signing_key(
    "runs/&lt;run-id&gt;/model",
    current_key,
    new_key,
)</code></pre>

  <h2>Read the release controls</h2>
  <p>A packaged run includes <code>provenance.json</code> for dataset, model, software, and environment identity; <code>sbom.json</code> for the deterministic CycloneDX component inventory; <code>release_attestation.json</code> for the canonical HMAC binding across those records; and <code>fault_injection_plan.json</code> for the release-blocking recovery scenarios. Set <code>SCML_SIGNING_KEY</code> in the release environment. The release evaluator verifies the attestation and blocks unsigned or tampered evidence. <code>FaultInjectionHarness</code> persists digest-bound scenario artifacts, while <code>LocalFaultExecutor</code> supplies bounded local execution evidence. External transport, container, and kernel-resource injectors remain a separate release requirement.</p>
  <p>For inference telemetry, use the redacted observability sidecar and evaluate it against an explicit policy. Missing required signals, stale or future timestamps, and threshold breaches must remain visible as failed evidence instead of being silently dropped.</p>

  <h2>Make reliability operable</h2>
  <p>An alert threshold is not an SLO until someone owns the response. <code>SLOTarget</code> binds a metric and worst-sample window to a minimum sample count, an owner, a runbook, and an alert route. Missing telemetry and future-dated samples are incident-visible states; they are never treated as healthy.</p>
  <pre><code>{`target = SLOTarget(
    name="prediction-latency",
    metric_name="p95_latency_ms",
    kind=SignalKind.LATENCY,
    maximum=250.0,
    window_days=7,
    minimum_samples=30,
    owner="ml-on-call",
    runbook="https://runbooks.example/prediction-latency",
    alert_route="on-call",
)
evaluation = SLOMonitor().evaluate(target, metrics, observed_at=now)`}</code></pre>
  <p>Use the result's status, remaining error budget, owner, runbook, and route in the incident record. The monitor uses the worst observation in the window so a breach cannot disappear inside an average.</p>

  <h2>Deliver the alert once</h2>
  <p><code>AlertDispatcher</code> keeps the SLO decision separate from delivery. It derives a stable alert identity from the evidence, records successful delivery, and deduplicates replays. Use the local JSONL sink for an operator handoff or the bounded HTTPS webhook sink for an authenticated incident gateway.</p>
  <pre><code>{`from scml.monitoring.alerts import Alert, AlertDispatcher, WebhookAlertSink

alert = Alert.from_evidence(
    route="on-call",
    severity="high",
    title="prediction latency SLO breached",
    details={"run_id": "run-42", "rollback_target": "model-previous"},
    evidence=evaluation.to_dict(),
    created_at=now,
)
delivery = AlertDispatcher(
    {"on-call": WebhookAlertSink("https://pager.example/alerts", token=token)},
    "runs/run-42/alert-deliveries.jsonl",
).dispatch(alert)`}</code></pre>
  <p>Retries are bounded and a failed webhook is recorded as failed rather than silently treated as a page. This proves the local routing contract; the organisation still needs to exercise its real pager, ticketing, authentication, and on-call escalation path.</p>

  <h2>Turn a breach into an incident</h2>
  <p>An alert is not a recovery plan. Open an incident with the affected run and the exact rollback target, then require an explicit acknowledgement before resolution. The journal is append-only, hash-chained, and rejects tampering; repeating a transition is idempotent.</p>
  <pre><code>{`from scml.monitoring.incidents import IncidentWorkflow

workflow = IncidentWorkflow("runs/&lt;run-id&gt;/incidents.jsonl")
incident = workflow.open(
    evaluation,
    opened_at=now,
    run_id="run-42",
    rollback_target="model-previous",
)
workflow.acknowledge(
    incident.incident_id,
    actor="ml-on-call",
    acknowledged_at=now,
)
workflow.resolve(
    incident.incident_id,
    actor="ml-on-call",
    resolved_at=resolved_at,
    resolution="rolled back and replayed the affected window",
    evidence_digest="sha256:...",
)`}</code></pre>
  <p>This local workflow supplies operator evidence; connect its alert route to the organisation's authenticated incident system before claiming external on-call coverage.</p>

  <h2>Join serving evidence to the run</h2>
  <p>Prediction telemetry tells you what happened; the evidence graph tells you what produced the bundle. Link an event only with the input and output fingerprints captured at the serving boundary and the exact bundle digest loaded for that request.</p>
  <pre><code>{`from scml.inference.evidence import InferenceEvidenceLinker

InferenceEvidenceLinker().link(
    "runs/&lt;run-id&gt;/evidence_graph.json",
    event_id="request-123",
    input_fingerprint="sha256:...",
    output_fingerprint="sha256:...",
    bundle_digest="sha256:...",
    timestamp="2026-10-20T12:00:00+00:00",
)`}</code></pre>
  <p>The linker verifies the graph and bundle identities, writes the graph atomically, and makes exact event replay idempotent. It does not replace redaction, retention, access control, or independent release review.</p>

  <h2>Classify the failure before you recover</h2>
  <p>Use the first trustworthy signal to decide which boundary failed. This prevents an operator from retraining around an infrastructure outage or rolling back a model when the source snapshot changed.</p>
  <div class="doc-cards">
    <div class="doc-card"><small>DATA FAILURE</small><h3>Stop before fit</h3><p>Signals include schema mismatch, blocking curation findings, duplicate-event conflicts, late-data policy violations, or a changed snapshot hash. Preserve the source and curation evidence; do not discard the offending rows or silently continue.</p></div>
    <div class="doc-card"><small>MODEL FAILURE</small><h3>Compare on locked data</h3><p>Signals include a metric regression, calibration or fairness breach, unstable explanation quality, or an error-budget breach tied to the bundle. Keep the champion, stage the challenger, and require an exact-bundle approval before promotion.</p></div>
    <div class="doc-card"><small>INFRASTRUCTURE FAILURE</small><h3>Resume or quarantine</h3><p>Signals include worker loss, timeout, disk or memory guard, partial write, stale checkpoint, or dependency mismatch. Verify the terminal state and artifact digest; resume only from a matching checkpoint or quarantine the incomplete run.</p></div>
    <div class="doc-card"><small>POLICY FAILURE</small><h3>Block the release</h3><p>Signals include missing signing evidence, expired approval, absent reviewer, unsupported capability, missing retention decision, or an unexecuted fault scenario. A policy failure is not fixed by a better score; it needs an explicit decision record.</p></div>
  </div>
  <pre><code>{`failure signal
      ↓ classify
data ───────→ preserve snapshot + curation evidence
model ──────→ retain champion + review challenger
infrastructure → verify terminal state + resume or quarantine
policy ─────→ block promotion + record disposition
      ↓
only a verified, digest-bound state may move forward`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Run the local fault matrix</h2>
  <p>The repository includes a bounded local executor for all 15 declared fault scenarios. It exercises real child-process termination, timeout cancellation, atomic publication, artifact quarantine, signature rejection, checkpoint and snapshot mismatch, retry exhaustion, duplicate-event idempotency, incomplete lineage, and rejected approvals.</p>
  <pre><code>{`from pathlib import Path

from scml.contracts import reliability
from scml.governance.local_faults import LocalFaultExecutor

executor = LocalFaultExecutor(Path("rehearsal/faults"))
for scenario in reliability.FaultScenario:
    artifacts = executor.execute(scenario)
    print(scenario.value, len(artifacts))`}</code></pre>
  <p>Pass the executor to <code>FaultInjectionHarness</code> to persist digest-bound evidence and update the release plan. The disk and memory cases use deterministic local capacity guards; they do not claim physical disk exhaustion or host-wide OOM coverage. Cloud transport outages and kernel-level resource tests require environment-specific injectors before a release can be marked production-proven.</p>

  <h2>Promote only an approved bundle</h2>
  <p>A better challenger is not automatically the new champion. The lifecycle stages it under the <code>challenger</code> alias. An independent reviewer must approve the exact model version and content digest, and the approval must be HMAC-signed, before the registry moves <code>champion</code>.</p>
  <pre><code>candidate = registry.resolve("customers", "challenger")
approval = PromotionApproval(
    model="customers",
    version=candidate.version,
    content_id=candidate.content_id,
    reviewer="reviewer@example.com",
    decision=PromotionDecision.APPROVED,
    reason="held-out, slice, explanation, and recovery evidence reviewed",
    evidence=("evidence/release-2026-10-20.json",),
    approved_at="2026-10-20T00:00:00+00:00",
).sign("release-key")
manager.promote_challenger(approval, signing_key="release-key")</code></pre>
  <p>The approval is bound to the bundle content, reviewer decision, evidence, and timestamp, then written to the append-only audit log. A missing, rejected, mismatched, tampered, or non-independent approval cannot move the serving alias. Rollback uses the same gate.</p>

  <h2>Deployment boundary</h2>
  <p>The built-in HTTP adapter is a reference server. Put it behind an authenticating gateway with TLS, request policy, logs, and operational ownership.</p>
  <p>For experimental modalities, <code>ModalityHttpServing</code> has the same deliberate boundary: it serves only a verified <code>ModalityPredictor</code> and a caller-owned <code>ModalitySampleDecoder</code>. The decoder is where transport-specific limits, privacy checks, codec policy, and raw-payload provenance belong.</p>
  <pre><code>{`server = ModalityHttpServing(
    predictor,
    decoder,
    max_request_bytes=16 * 1024 * 1024,
    max_samples=10_000,
)
server.start()
try:
    wait_for_shutdown()
finally:
    server.stop()`}</code></pre>
  <p><code>/health</code> reports the verified package and modality identity; <code>/schema</code> reports the decoder contract; <code>/predict</code> accepts <code>&#123;&quot;samples&quot;: ...&#125;</code>. This is not an implicit codec registry and it does not provide authentication or distributed serving by itself.</p>
  <div class="callout"><p><strong>Release rehearsal:</strong> before promotion, replay a known input, verify the signed bundle, confirm the serving schema, exercise resume from a checkpoint, inspect monitoring output, and record who reviewed the evidence. A green training run is not by itself a release decision.</p></div>
</DocsLayout>
